# litkg-rs Agent-First Backend Upgradability Plan

## Summary

Improve litkg-rs by making it an **agent context/action compiler first**, not a backend zoo. The next slice should make every source/backend expose a small static capability contract, then let `litkg context-pack` produce task-specific evidence, next commands, missing-context leaves, and risk flags that Codex and Gemini can both use.

Success means: for broad ARIA-NBV coding/docs/thesis tasks, agents know when to run litkg-rs, get a concise action pack, and can see which configured backends are ready, stale, missing, or unsafe without guessing.

## Key Changes

- Add a static backend/source contract in `litkg-core`:
  - `SourceDescriptor`: name, kind, configured paths, supported node/edge types, freshness inputs, required env/tools.
  - `BackendDescriptor`: graphify, Neo4j export, CodeGraphContext, Graphiti, Context7, OpenAI docs, Semantic Scholar, MemPalace as named capabilities.
  - `CapabilityProbe`: read-only readiness check returning `ready | configured | stale | missing | unavailable` plus exact repair command.
  - `ConformanceReport`: deterministic JSON used by tests and agents.

- Upgrade `litkg capabilities --format json`:
  - include all configured sources/backends from `.configs/litkg.toml`;
  - report `agent_recommendation`: `use_now`, `refresh_first`, `missing_leaf`, or `do_not_use`;
  - include exact commands such as `make kg-materialize`, `make kg-index-code`, or `litkg context-pack ...`;
  - never require live runtime checks unless `--check-runtime` is passed.

- Upgrade `litkg context-pack` into the primary agent surface:
  - keep `--profile agents-scaffold`;
  - add ARIA task profiles: `thesis-coding`, `docs-paper-sync`, `rri-oracle`, `vin-baseline`, `rollout-planning`;
  - JSON output must include: `task_summary`, `action_plan`, `evidence_spans`, `relevant_symbols`, `relevant_papers`, `active_backlog`, `missing_leaves`, `risk_flags`, `verification_commands`, and `backend_status`;
  - text output mirrors the same sections for humans;
  - budget behavior remains deterministic and approximate.

- Make agent adoption explicit in ARIA-NBV:
  - root `AGENTS.md`: require `litkg context-pack` for broad cross-surface coding/docs/KG/thesis tasks; exempt localized one-file edits.
  - `semantic-scholar-litkg` skill: document the shared Codex/Gemini contract and exact context-pack examples.
  - Do not add separate Codex/Gemini workflows. Use one CLI/JSON contract plus tiny setup notes.

- Motivate agents to use it efficiently:
  - action-pack output should start with the smallest useful next step, not a long graph dump;
  - every missing backend/doc source should appear as a “leaf” with a concrete resolution command;
  - include “why this pack matters” risk flags: stale docs, missing generated KG, unresolved bibliography, no code index, dirty agent backlog;
  - include copy-paste-ready commands and file paths so the agent gets immediate task momentum.

## Public Interfaces

- CLI:
  - `litkg capabilities --config <path> --format json [--check-runtime]`
  - `litkg context-pack --config <path> --repo-root <path> --task "<task>" --budget <n> --profile <profile> --format json|text`

- Config:
  - keep current `.configs/litkg.toml` shape;
  - add optional per-source/backend labels only if needed for profile routing;
  - no dynamic plugin loading in this slice.

- Rust:
  - static traits/structs in `litkg-core`; existing graphify/Neo4j/Semantic Scholar code implements descriptors/probes.
  - no MCP server behavior yet. MCP later wraps `capabilities` and `context-pack`.

## Test Plan

- Unit tests:
  - each backend descriptor serializes deterministically;
  - readiness states distinguish configured, ready, stale, missing, unavailable;
  - context-pack JSON includes required top-level fields for each new profile;
  - missing Context7/OpenAI-doc/code-index leaves are stable and actionable.

